# TypeScript Style Rules

## Conditions

- Always use explicit comparisons.
- Prefer `value !== null` over `!value`.
- Prefer `value === undefined` over falsy checks when checking for undefined.
- Avoid implicit truthiness checks unless the value is explicitly boolean.

## Code Style

- Write clear, predictable and strongly typed TypeScript.
- Prefer explicit intent over shorter syntax.
- Keep public APIs strongly typed.
- Avoid `any` unless explicitly requested by the user.

## Type strictness
- `strict`, `noUnusedLocals`, and `noImplicitOverride` are enabled — never
  weaken these in `tsconfig.json` to make code compile.
- Avoid `any`. If a third-party type is genuinely unknown, use `unknown` and
  narrow it, or add a short comment explaining why `any` is unavoidable in
  that specific spot.

## Naming
- Classes/types: PascalCase. Functions/variables: camelCase. File names
  follow the existing casing already used in each directory (do not introduce
  a new casing convention in a directory that has an established one).
- Boolean-returning functions/methods read as a predicate:
  `isPossibleToMakeMove`, `isPossibleToMakeMove()` — not `checkMove` or
  `moveStatus`.

## CSS / DOM (outside the canvas)
- Prefer CSS custom properties for values that scale with layout (already the
  pattern for field/tile sizing) over hardcoded pixel values in TS.
